Refactor this code by extracting the repeated unit conversion:

def c_to_f(c):
    return c * 9 / 5 + 32

def f_to_c(f):
    return (f - 32) * 5 / 9

def k_to_c(k):
    return k - 273.15

def c_to_k(c):
    return c + 273.15

def f_to_k(f):
    return (f - 32) * 5 / 9 + 273.15

output:
def c_to_f(c):
    return c * 9 / 5 + 32

def f_to_c(f):
    return (f - 32) * 5 / 9

def c_to_k(c):
    return c + 273.15

def k_to_c(k):
    return k - 273.15

def f_to_k(f):
    return c_to_k(f_to_c(f))

def k_to_f(k):
    return c_to_f(k_to_c(k))

What changed: conversions were built from two primitives (c_to_f, c_to_k) instead of re-deriving formulas, so a new unit only needs one canonical function.

